  "text": "We are given a triangle $ \\triangle ABC $ inscribed in a circle (i.e., a **circle circumscribed** around the triangle), with sides:\n\n- $ AB = 5 \\, \\text{cm} $\n- $ AC = 12 \\, \\text{cm} $\n\nWe are also told that a point $ D $ lies on the circle such that:\n\n- $ BD = 20 \\, \\text{cm} $\n- $ CD = 8 \\, \\text{cm} $\n\nWe are to find the **greatest possible area** of triangle $ \\triangle ADB $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the Law of Cosines or Law of Sines\n\nLet’s denote the **circle** as having radius $ R $. The triangle $ \\triangle ABC $ is inscribed in the circle, so we can use the **Law of Cosines** to relate the sides of the triangle to the angles or the radius.\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n- $ \\angle A = \\alpha $\n- $ \\angle B = \\beta $\n- $ \\angle C = \\gamma $\n\nThen:\n\n$$\nAB^2 + AC^2 - 2 \\cdot AB \\cdot AC \\cdot \\cos(\\alpha) = BC^2\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nBC^2 = AB^2 + AC^2 - 2 \\cdot AB \\cdot AC \\cdot \\cos(\\alpha)\n$$\n\n$$\nBC^2 = 5^2 + 12^2 - 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 12 \\cdot \\cos(\\alpha) = 25 + 144 - 120 \\cos(\\alpha)\n$$\n\n$$\nBC^2 = 169 - 120 \\cos(\\alpha)\n$$\n\nNow, using the Law of Sines:\n\n$$\n\\frac{BC}{\\sin(\\alpha)} = \\frac{AB}{\\sin(\\gamma)} = 2R\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\gamma) = \\frac{BC}{2R}, \\quad \\cos(\\alpha) = \\frac{AB}{2R}\n$$\n\nBut we don’t yet know $ R $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the Power of a Point\n\nPoint $ D $ lies on the circle, so we can use the **Power of a Point** theorem:\n\n$$\nBD \\cdot DC = AD \\cdot DC\n$$\n\nGiven:\n\n- $ BD = 20 $\n- $ DC = 8 $\n\nThen:\n\n$$\nBD \\cdot DC = 20 \\cdot 8 = 160\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nAD \\cdot 8 = 160 \\Rightarrow AD = \\frac{160}{8} = 20 \\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nSo, triangle $ \\triangle ADB $ has sides:\n\n- $ AD = 20 $\n- $ BD = 20 $\n- $ AB = 5 $\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\triangle ADB \\text{ has two sides equal to } 20 \\text{ cm and one side } 5 \\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the Area of $ \\triangle ADB $\n\nWe can use ** Heron’s formula** to find the area.\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n- $ a = 20 $\n- $ b = 20 $\n- $ c = 5 $\n\nThen:\n\n$$\ns = \\frac{a + b + c}{2} = \\frac{20 + 20 + 5}{2} = 22.5\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area} = \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)} = \\sqrt{22.5 \\cdot (22.5 - 20)(22.5 - 20)(22.5 - 5)}\n$$\n\n$$\n= \\sqrt{22.5 \\cdot 2.5 \\cdot 2.5 \\cdot 17.5}\n$$\n\n$$\n= \\sqrt{22.5 \\cdot 2.5^2 \\cdot 17.5}\n= 2.5 \\cdot \\sqrt{22.5 \\cdot 17.5}\n$$\n\nNow compute:\n\n$$\n22.5 \\cdot 17.5 = (15 + 7.5)(15 - 7.5) = 15^2 - (7.5)^2 = 225 - 56.25 = 168.75\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area} = 2.5 \\cdot \\sqrt{168.75}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{168.75} \\approx 13 \\Rightarrow \\text{Area} \\approx 2.5 \\cdot 13 = 32.5\n$$\n\nSo the **maximum possible area** of $ \\triangle ADB $ is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{32.5} \\, \\text{cm}^2\n$$\n\n--- \n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{32.5} \\, \\text{cm}^2\n$$",
